
/*
BACAP - Blinded Cryptographic Capability System

Overview
--------
BACAP enables a Writer (Alice) to post an infinite sequence of
single-use message boxes that only a designated Reader (Bob) can
decrypt, but any observer can verify a box's signature. The boxes are signed with 
a per-box blinded key derived from a root key, while the messages are encrypted with 
per-box symmetric keys. 

Two capability types:
  WriteCap  - (skR, Hj, j): root Ed25519 private key + initial HKDF state and index. 
              We will simplify this to (skR, H0). 
              Lets the holder derive BoxIDs, encrypt/decrypt payloads, and sign boxes.

  ReadCap   - (pkR=pk(skR), Hj, j): root Ed25519 public key + initial HKDF state and index.
              We will simplify this to (pkR, H0).
              Lets the holder derive BoxIDs, and encrypt/decrypt payloads.

Alice creates both the WriteCap and ReadCap, and shares the ReadCap with Bob out-of-band.

HKDF chain:
  HKDF(H_i, i) -> H_{i+1} || E_i || K_i
    H_{i+1}  next HKDF state
    E_i      symmetric encryption key
    K_i      blinding factor (scalar for Ed25519 key blinding)

Per-message derivation (with optional context value ctx):
  K_i^ctx  = HKDF(K_i, ctx)                   context-specific blinding factor
  E_i^ctx  = HKDF(E_i, ctx)                   context-specific encryption key
  BoxID_i  = Blind(pkR, K_i^ctx)              blinded public key (for both signature verification and indexing messages)
  ct_i     = AESGCMSIV(E_i^ctx, msg)          the ciphertext (AES-GCM-SIV encrypted message payload)
  sig_i    = Sign(Blind(skR,K_i^ctx), ct_i)   an Ed25519 signature over ct_i by the blinded private key

A box/message is (BoxID_i, ct_i, sig_i). These values are public and transmitted by Alice 
to the network, and so anyone can verify that a box is valid by checking the signature. To receive the message, 
Bob computes the BoxID_i, verifies the signature using BoxID_i as the public key, 
and decrypts ct_i using E_i^ctx derived from his ReadCap.

Signing with Blind(skR,K_i^ctx) can be verified with Blind(pkR, K_i^ctx) by the Ed25519 blinding property: 
the blinded key pair is a valid key pair, so standard verify/sign semantics apply at the blinded level.

Lemmae:
    - session_exists         - executability / sanity check
    - message_secrecy        - adversary cannot read ct_i without ReadCap
    - message_authenticity   - every valid box was created by the WriteCap holder
    - read_cap_cannot_forge  - ReadCap holders cannot forge a valid box
    - forward_secrecy        - HKDF chain state snapshot does not expose past messages
    - group_messaging_exists - two distinct readers can both receive the same box
*/

theory BACAP
begin

builtins: signing, symmetric-encryption

/*
We establish the following functions:

hkdfH(H_i) = H_{i+1}: iterating to the next HKDF state
hkdfE(H_i) = E_i    : symmetric encryption key for the current step
hkdfK(H_i) = K_i    : blinding factor for the current step (Ed25519 scalar)

ctxK(K_i, ctx) = K_i^ctx: context-specific blinding factor
ctxE(E_i, ctx) = E_i^ctx: context-specific encryption key

Ed25519 key blinding (EC multiplication):
   blindPk(pkR, K_i^ctx)  = Blind(pkR, K_i^ctx): blinded public key (= BoxID)
   blindSk(skR, K_i^ctx) = Blind(skR, K_i^ctx): blinded private key (used to sign)
*/


functions: hkdfH/1, hkdfE/1, hkdfK/1,
           ctxK/2, ctxE/2,
           blindPk/2, blindSk/2

  

equations:
    verify(sign(m, blindSk(sk, k)), m, blindPk(pk(sk), k)) = true


/*
Create a WriteCap for party A.
   Fr(~skR) - fresh Ed25519 root private key
   Fr(~H0)  - fresh initial HKDF state
We need the WriteCap to persist.
*/

rule CreateWriteCap:
    [ Fr(~skR)
    , Fr(~H0)
    ]
  --[ CreateWriteCap($A, pk(~skR)) ]->
    [ !WriteCap($A, ~skR, ~H0) ]

/*
Derive and share a ReadCap from the WriteCap.

ReadCap contains the public key pkR = pk(~skR) and the initial HKDF state ~H0.
This models Bob receiving (pkR, H0) out-of-band from Alice.
We can give the !ReadCap to multiple users to initiate a sequence of messages we would write to a group chat.
*/


rule ShareReadCap:
    [ !WriteCap($A, ~skR, ~H0) ]
  --[ ShareReadCap($A, $B, pk(~skR)) ]->
    [ !ReadCap($B, $A, pk(~skR), ~H0) ]

/*
Initialise Alice's write-state chain from her WriteCap.
WriteState is consumed and re-emitted by Writer_Send, modelling the advance of MessageBoxIndex.
Executing Writer_Init again from the same !WriteCap creates an independent second chain starting from H0, 
enabling multiple readers for the message sequance (e.g. in a group chat).
*/

rule Writer_Init:
    [ !WriteCap($A, ~skR, ~H0) ]
  --[ WriteStep($A, ~H0) ]->
    [ WriteState($A, ~skR, ~H0) ]


/*
Writer sends a message box at the current HKDF state Hi.
     Ki     = hkdfK(Hi)          K_i: blinding factor from HKDF
     Ei     = hkdfE(Hi)          E_i: encryption key from HKDF
     Kctx   = ctxK(Ki, 'ctx')    K_i^ctx: context-specific blinding
     Ectx   = ctxE(Ei, 'ctx')    E_i^ctx: context-specific enc. key
     boxID  = blindPk(pk(~skR), Kctx)   BoxID doubles as verif. key
     ct     = senc(~msg, Ectx)   ciphertext (AES-GCM-SIV in Go)
     sig    = sign(ct, blindSk(~skR, Kctx))  signed with blinded sk

   State advances: WriteState consumes Hi, re-emits hkdfH(Hi) = H_{i+1}
   Posts the box to the network visible to the adversary.
*/


rule Writer_Send:
    let Ki    = hkdfK(Hi)
        Ei    = hkdfE(Hi)
        Kctx  = ctxK(Ki,  'ctx')
        Ectx  = ctxE(Ei,  'ctx')
        boxID = blindPk(pk(~skR), Kctx)
        ct    = senc(~msg, Ectx)
        sig   = sign(ct, blindSk(~skR, Kctx))
    in
    [ WriteState($A, ~skR, Hi)
    , Fr(~msg)
    ]
  --[ Sent($A, boxID, ~msg)
    , WriteStep($A, hkdfH(Hi))
    ]->
    [ WriteState($A, ~skR, hkdfH(Hi))
    , Out(<boxID, ct, sig>)
    ]

/*
Initialise Bob's read-state chain from his ReadCap.
*/


rule Reader_Init:
    [ !ReadCap($B, $A, pkR, H0) ]
  -->
    [ ReadState($B, $A, pkR, H0) ]


/*
Reader fetches and processes one message box at current HKDF state Hi:
Compute BoxID, fetch ct=senc(msg, Ectx) and sig from network, verify signature, decrypt ciphertext.
State advances to hkdfH(Hi), keeping reader in sync with writer.
*/

rule Reader_Recv:
    let Kctx  = ctxK(hkdfK(Hi), 'ctx')
        Ectx  = ctxE(hkdfE(Hi), 'ctx')
        boxID = blindPk(pkR, Kctx)
    in
    [ ReadState($B, $A, pkR, Hi)
    , In(<senc(msg, Ectx), sig>)
    ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sig, senc(msg, Ectx), boxID), true)
    , Recv($B, $A, boxID, msg)
    ]->
    [ ReadState($B, $A, pkR, hkdfH(Hi)) ]



// Make sure the cases where signature verification fails are pruned.

restriction Eq:
    "All x y #i. Eq(x, y) @ i ==> x = y"


/*
Model WriteCap compromise at time 0. 
Leaking skR will allow the adversary to derive all BoxIDs, encrypt, sign, and
decrypt.
*/    
  

rule RevealWriteCap:
    [ !WriteCap($A, ~skR, ~H0) ]
  --[ WriteCapReveal($A) ]->
    [ Out(<~skR, ~H0>) ]


/*
Model ReadCap compromise at time 0. 
Leaking pkR + H0 will allow the adversary to derive all BoxIDs, encrypt, and decrypt
ciphertexts, but not forge signatures.
*/


rule RevealReadCap:
    [ !ReadCap($B, $A, pkR, H0) ]
  --[ ReadCapReveal($B, $A) ]->
    [ Out(<pkR, H0>) ]


/*
Model a snapshot compromise of the writer's current HKDF chain state at time i.
The adversary learns Hi, enabling it to compute all future states and derived keys,
but not past states, since hkdfH has no defined inverse. 
The WriteState is re-emitted unchanged to model a passive memory read.
*/


rule RevealWriteState:
    [ WriteState($A, ~skR, Hi) ]
  --[ WriteStateReveal($A, Hi) ]->
    [ WriteState($A, ~skR, Hi)
    , Out(Hi)
    ]

/*
Sources lemma necessary to break a RevealWriteState re-emission cycle.
*/

lemma writestate_source [sources]:
  "All A Hi #r.
    WriteStateReveal(A, Hi) @ #r
    ==>
    Ex #s. WriteStep(A, Hi) @ #s & #s < #r
  "



// Security Lemmas
// ===============




/*
Executability / sanity check

A trace is found where:
   - Alice sent a box with some message under boxID
   - Bob received and successfully decrypted the same boxID and message
   - Neither the WriteCap nor ReadCap was compromised
*/

lemma session_exists:
  exists-trace
  "
    Ex A B boxID msg #i #j.
      Sent(A, boxID, msg) @ #i
    & Recv(B, A, boxID, msg) @ #j
    & not(Ex #r. WriteCapReveal(A) @ r)
    & not(Ex #r. ReadCapReveal(B, A) @ r)
  "

/*
Message secrecy

The Dolev-Yao adversary cannot learn the plaintext ~msg of any box sent by writer A, provided
it has neither the WriteCap nor the ReadCap for A.

There does not exist a trace wehere the adversary learns msg at some time j that Alice sends at time i, 
while there is no time r where A's WriteCap was revealed, and no time r where any B's ReadCap for A was revealed.
*/

lemma message_secrecy:
  "
    not(
      Ex A boxID msg #i #j.
        Sent(A, boxID, msg) @ #i
      & K(msg) @ #j
      & not(Ex #r. WriteCapReveal(A) @ r)
      & not(Ex B #r. ReadCapReveal(B, A) @ r)
    )
  "

/*
Message authenticity

Every box that passes signature verification was created by a WriteCap holder.

   Recv(B, A, boxID, msg) @ #i  - Bob accepted a box at some time i
   Sent(A, boxID, msg)   @ #j   - Alice actually sent that box at some time j

If Bob accepts a box with boxID and msg at time i, then either:
  - Alice actually sent that box with that boxID and msg at some time j, or
  - some WriteCap was revealed at some time r, enabling the adversary to forge messages.
*/

lemma message_authenticity:
  "
    All B A boxID msg #i.
      Recv(B, A, boxID, msg) @ #i
      ==>
        ( Ex #j. Sent(A, boxID, msg) @ #j )
      | ( Ex #r. WriteCapReveal(A) @ r )
  "

/*
Read-cap holders cannot forge a message

A ReadCap holder knows (pkR, H0) and can derive all BoxIDs and decrypt
all ciphertexts. However, signing requires blindSk(skR, Kctx), which
needs the root private key skR - not derivable from pkR alone.
*/


lemma read_cap_cannot_forge:
  "
    All B A boxID msg #i.
      Recv(B, A, boxID, msg) @ #i
    & (Ex #r. ReadCapReveal(B, A) @ r)
    ==>
        ( Ex #j. Sent(A, boxID, msg) @ #j )
      | ( Ex #r. WriteCapReveal(A) @ r )
  "

/* Forward secrecy

BACAP inherits computational post-quantum forward-security from the irreversibility of the KDF function.

RevealWriteState models a snapshot compromise of the live chain state Hi.
The adversary learns Hi and can compute all future states hkdfH(Hi), hkdfH(hkdfH(Hi)),... 
But cannot recover any H_{r}, for r<i used in the past.
*/

lemma forward_secrecy:
  "All A boxID msg #i.
    Sent(A, boxID, msg) @ #i 
    ==>
    not (Ex #j. K(msg) @ #j)
    | (Ex Hi #r. WriteStateReveal(A, Hi) @ #r & #r < #i)
    | (Ex #w. WriteCapReveal(A) @ #w)
    | (Ex B #c. ReadCapReveal(B, A) @ #c)
  "

  
/*
Group messaging executability

Two distinct readers holding a ReadCap for the same sequence can both successfully 
receive and decrypt the same box.
*/

lemma group_messaging_exists:
  exists-trace
  "
    Ex A B1 B2 boxID msg #i #j #k.
      Sent(A, boxID, msg) @ #i
    & Recv(B1, A, boxID, msg) @ #j
    & Recv(B2, A, boxID, msg) @ #k
    & not(B1 = B2)
    & not(Ex #r. WriteCapReveal(A) @ r)
    & not(Ex #r. ReadCapReveal(B1, A) @ r)
    & not(Ex #r. ReadCapReveal(B2, A) @ r)
  "

end